export default "# Issue: Approve the commit message before committing\n\n**Source**: DW-3 (manual description)\n**Source type**: manual\n**Parent**: none\n**Additional context**: none\n**Date**: 2026-08-22\n**Branch**: feature/DW-3_commit-message-approval-gate\n**Base branch**: main\n**Status**: Clarified \u2014 awaiting planning\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe three commit-producing skills \u2014 `/workflow-commit`, `/workflow-commit-dev`, and\n`/workflow-release` \u2014 compose a commit message and then commit it without ever putting the\nmessage in front of the user for approval. The user wants to see the message and be able to\naccept, change, or abandon it before anything lands.\n\n**The premise of the report needs one correction.** This is not a regression from the\nconventional-commits change. Comparing the three skills at `82430f7` (their original state)\nagainst `HEAD` shows that no message-approval gate has ever existed:\n\n- `workflow-commit` at `82430f7` ran compose (step 4) \u2192 dry-run gate (step 5) \u2192 commit\n  (step 6). No approval. Today it runs compose (5) \u2192 breaking (6) \u2192 dry-run gate (7) \u2192\n  commit (8). Still no approval.\n- `allowed-tools` already carried `Bash(git commit:*)` in all three skills at `82430f7`, so\n  the Claude Code permission prompt \u2014 which would have shown the `-m \"\u2026\"` text and served as\n  an incidental approval moment \u2014 has been suppressed from the beginning. The\n  conventional-commits change added only `Bash(ls:*)` (needed for the commitlint probe).\n- `workflow-release` has a review step, but it is *post*-commit (\u00a76 Step 2) and amends with\n  `git commit --amend --no-edit`, which reuses the subject verbatim. Lines 151\u2013153 of that\n  file state this outright. The changelog and the staged files are reviewable there; the\n  message never has been.\n\nWhat did change is that the skills got substantially more prescriptive \u2014 a detection step, a\ntype-selection table, a breaking-change step \u2014 so the model now marches through numbered\nsteps and no longer pauses to narrate the message on its own. The incidental pause the user\nremembers was model behaviour, not a skill instruction, which is exactly why it disappeared\nwithout anything being deleted.\n\nSo the fix is to **add** a gate that was never specified, not to restore one that was removed.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nBefore any of the three skills runs `git commit`, it stops and shows the user:\n\n- the composed message in full (subject, plus the `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer when present),\n- which style produced it (conventional or house) and how that was decided,\n- the staged file list,\n- the exact tag that will be created, for `/workflow-commit-dev` and `/workflow-release`,\n- whether a push will follow (`--push`, or release's own push step).\n\nIt then waits for one of three answers:\n\n- **yes** \u2192 commit exactly what was shown.\n- **edit** \u2192 take the replacement subject the user types, use it **verbatim** as the whole\n  subject line, and commit that.\n- **no** \u2192 commit nothing. Leave the staged changes staged and stop.\n\nThe gate is unconditional: there is no flag to skip it. `--dry-run` is unaffected, because it\nalready stops before the commit and therefore never reaches the gate.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Given staged changes and no `--dry-run`, When `/workflow-commit` reaches the commit\n      step, Then it displays the composed message, the detected style, and the staged file\n      list, and waits for an answer before running `git commit`.\n- [ ] Same for `/workflow-commit-dev`.\n- [ ] Same for `/workflow-release`, positioned before \u00a76 Step 1's initial commit.\n- [ ] Given the gate is showing, When the user answers **yes**, Then the commit is made with\n      the exact message displayed \u2014 no re-composition.\n- [ ] Given the gate is showing, When the user answers **edit** and types a replacement,\n      Then that text becomes the entire subject line verbatim, with no prefix or `type(scope):`\n      re-applied on top of it.\n- [ ] Given the gate is showing, When the user answers **no**, Then no commit and no tag is\n      created, the staged changes remain staged, and the skill stops and says so.\n- [ ] Given `--dry-run`, When the skill runs, Then the existing dry-run preview stops it and\n      the approval gate is never reached \u2014 the user is not prompted twice.\n- [ ] Given `/workflow-commit-dev` on an `RC-*` or `test-RC-*` branch, When the gate displays,\n      Then it shows the fully resolved tag (`dev-rc{n}-{version}`), not the template.\n- [ ] Given `/workflow-release`, When the gate displays, Then the message already includes any\n      `!` and `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, since \u00a76 amends with `--no-edit` and cannot add them later.\n- [ ] Given a description override was passed to `/workflow-commit`, When the gate displays,\n      Then it still fires \u2014 an override feeds composition, it does not authorise the commit.\n- [ ] `/workflow-release`'s existing post-commit amend loop (\u00a76 Step 2) is left intact.\n- [ ] The gate text is written inline in all three SKILL.md files, consistent with the existing\n      detection block; no shared reference file is introduced.\n- [ ] `/workflow-help` and `README.md` describe the gate wherever they describe the three\n      skills' shared commit behaviour.\n- [ ] `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` reads `\"version\": \"1.7.2\"`. Root `plugin.json` is untouched \u2014\n      it does not exist on this branch (DW-2's deliverable), so the \"keep both manifests\n      identical\" rule has nothing to enforce here. DW-2 retains 1.8.0 on its own branch.\n\n## Affected Components\n\n- `skills/workflow-commit/SKILL.md` \u2014 new gate between step 7 (dry-run) and step 8 (commit);\n  renumber the steps that follow.\n- `skills/workflow-commit-dev/SKILL.md` \u2014 new gate between step 9 (dry-run) and step 10\n  (commit); the display must cover the step 8 tag command as well.\n- `skills/workflow-release/SKILL.md` \u2014 new gate inside \u00a76, ahead of Step 1's initial commit;\n  must show the assembled message from \u00a73 and the \u00a76 Step 3 tag.\n- `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 the COMMIT HELPERS block and the per-step table describe\n  the shared commit behaviour and will understate it otherwise.\n- `README.md` \u2014 the **Commit format** paragraph is the other place the three skills' shared\n  behaviour is documented.\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and `plugin.json` \u2014 version bump, kept identical.\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Scenarios\n\n- **Double-prompting on `--dry-run`.** The dry-run gate already prints the message and stops.\n  The approval gate must sit *after* it in the step order so the two never both fire.\n- **Edit re-prefixing.** If the edited text is passed back through composition, a user typing\n  `fix(DW-3): restore the gate` gets `fix(DW-3): fix(DW-3): restore the gate`. The edited\n  string replaces the whole subject and is not re-processed.\n- **Edit that breaks the detected convention.** The user may type something commitlint would\n  reject. Take it verbatim anyway \u2014 the gate exists to give the user the last word \u2014 but it is\n  worth one line of warning when a conventional repo receives a non-conventional edit.\n- **Cancel leaves state.** `git add -A` has already run by the time the gate fires. Cancelling\n  must not unstage; the user's likely next move is to re-run.\n- **Two questions in one run.** In a repo with no detectable convention, the detection step\n  already asks `Use Conventional Commits? (y/N)`. The approval gate makes two prompts in a\n  single `/workflow-commit`. That is acceptable \u2014 they decide different things \u2014 but the gate\n  should not restate what the detection step just settled.\n- **Release with an existing tag.** \u00a77 already stops on a duplicate tag. Because the gate now\n  advertises the tag it is about to create, that check reads better before the gate than after.\n- **Nothing staged.** Each skill already stops earlier; the gate is unreachable and needs no\n  guard of its own.\n\n## Dependencies & Blockers\n\n- None external.\n- DW-2 (Agent Plugins compatibility) was committed as `d25d4b8` on\n  `feature/DW-2_agent-plugins-compatibility` before this clarification, so `main` is clean.\n  That branch is unmerged and also touches `README.md` and both manifests \u2014 the same files\n  DW-3 will edit \u2014 so expect to resolve that overlap at merge time.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nAll resolved during clarification:\n\n1. **Gate form** \u2014 approve / edit / cancel, not a plain yes-no. Retyping a wrong scope should\n   not cost a full re-run.\n2. **Skippable** \u2014 no. The gate is unconditional; no `--yes` flag. `--dry-run` bypasses it only\n   because it stops first.\n3. **Release placement** \u2014 gate goes before \u00a76 Step 1's initial commit; the existing post-commit\n   amend loop stays. They settle different things: the gate settles the message, the loop\n   settles the changelog and files.\n4. **Tag coverage** \u2014 the tag is shown inside the same gate as the message, approved together.\n   One prompt, and a wrong pushed tag is the messiest of the three to undo.\n5. **Duplication** \u2014 write the gate inline in all three skills, matching the existing treatment\n   of the detection block. Self-contained SKILL.md files travel to non-Claude agents; a sibling\n   reference file may not.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n**Mode**: Regular\n**Complexity**: Medium\n**Steps**: 7\n\n### The canonical gate block\n\nSteps 1\u20133 insert *this same text*, adapted only where a skill has no tag. Compose it once, paste\nit three times. Divergence between the three copies is the failure this plan is most likely to\nproduce, and nothing in the repo will catch it.\n\n```markdown\nN. **Approve the message.** Before committing, show exactly what is about to happen, then wait:\n\n   ```\n   Proposed commit message:\n\n     <composed message, including any BREAKING CHANGE: footer>\n\n   Style:  <conventional | house> \u2014 <how it was decided>\n   Staged: <n> file(s)\n     <file list>\n   Tag:    <exact tag to be created>        (omit this line in /workflow-commit)\n   Push:   <yes, to <upstream> | no>\n\n   Commit this? (yes / edit / no)\n   ```\n\n   - **yes** \u2192 commit exactly the message shown. Do not recompose it.\n   - **edit** \u2192 take the replacement typed by the developer and use it **verbatim** as the whole\n     subject line. Do not re-apply the `TASK-ID:` prefix or the `type(scope):` wrapper \u2014 the text\n     they typed already *is* the subject, and re-wrapping it yields `fix(DW-3): fix(DW-3): \u2026`.\n     If the edit does not match a convention the repo enforces, say so once and take it anyway.\n   - **no** \u2192 commit nothing, create no tag. Leave the staged changes staged and stop.\n\n   Ask every time. No flag skips this. `--dry-run` never arrives here \u2014 it stopped one step ago.\n```\n\n### Step 1: Gate `/workflow-commit`\n- **Action**: Insert the gate as a new step 8, between the existing step 7 (dry-run gate) and\n  step 8 (commit). Omit the `Tag:` line. Renumber the tail: commit 8\u21929, push 9\u219210, summary 10\u219211.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-commit/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Why**: The most-used of the three, and the one the report was filed against.\n- **Verify**: Steps read 1\u202611 with no repeats or gaps; the gate sits after the dry-run gate, so\n  the two can never both fire; step 5's \"compose\" language still flows into it.\n\n### Step 2: Gate `/workflow-commit-dev`\n- **Action**: Insert the gate as a new step 10, between step 9 (dry-run gate) and step 10\n  (commit). Populate `Tag:` from step 8's resolution \u2014 the **resolved** value\n  (`dev-rc2-1.9.0`), never the `dev-{VersionValue}` template. Renumber: commit 10\u219211, tag 11\u219212,\n  push 12\u219213, summary 13\u219214.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-commit-dev/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: Old step 11 (\"Tag using the command from step 8\") still points at step 8 \u2014 tag\n  *determination* sits before the gate and must not be renumbered. Confirm that cross-reference\n  survived; it is the one dangling pointer this renumbering can create.\n\n### Step 3: Gate `/workflow-release`\n- **Action**: Add `#### Step 1 \u2014 Approve the message and tag` at the top of \u00a76 (line 198),\n  pushing the existing Steps 1/2/3 to 2/3/4. Draw the message from \u00a73 Step 4 and the tag from\n  what is now Step 4. Also move the **\"Tag already exists\"** check out of \u00a77 (line 234) to run\n  *before* the gate.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-release/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Why**: The release commit is the one that gets tagged and pushed, and today its message is\n  the least approvable of the three \u2014 \u00a76's amend loop uses `--amend --no-edit`, which reuses the\n  subject verbatim (the file says so at lines 151\u2013153).\n- **Verify**: The gate precedes the initial commit; the existing post-commit amend loop is\n  untouched; a gate advertising a tag can no longer promise one that \u00a77 would have rejected.\n\n### Step 4: Update `/workflow-help`\n- **Action**: Add a line to the COMMIT HELPERS block (lines 108\u2013126) stating that all three\n  skills show the message and wait for yes/edit/no. Update the `commit`, `commit-dev`, and\n  `release` rows of the per-step table.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: The block already documents shared detection behaviour; the gate now sits beside\n  it. Keep inside the box-drawing width used by the rest of the file.\n\n### Step 5: Update `README.md`\n- **Action**: Extend the **Commit format** paragraph (lines 49\u201353) with a sentence on the gate.\n- **Files**: `README.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: Reads as one paragraph about shared behaviour, not a changelog entry.\n\n### Step 6: Bump the version to 1.7.2\n- **Action**: Set `\"version\"` in `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` from `1.7.1` to **`1.7.2`**\n  (decided by the developer). Root `plugin.json` is **not** touched \u2014 it does not exist on this\n  branch, being DW-2's deliverable, so the \"keep both manifests identical\" rule has nothing to\n  enforce here. DW-2 keeps 1.8.0 on its branch; 1.7.2 sorts below it, which stays coherent if\n  DW-3 merges first.\n- **Files**: `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 modify (one line)\n- **Why**: A patch bump records this as corrective \u2014 the behaviour the developer expected was\n  missing. Note the tension worth keeping in view: the branch is `feature/` and the analysis\n  concluded the gate is new capability, so a conventional-commit `feat:` on a patch bump will\n  read slightly against itself. Deliberate, not an oversight.\n- **Verify**: `grep '\"version\"' .claude-plugin/plugin.json` shows 1.7.2; `git diff main --stat`\n  lists only the three skills, `workflow-help`, `README.md`, and this one manifest.\n\n### Step 7: Verify the three copies match\n- **Action**: Diff the three inserted gate blocks against each other and confirm they differ\n  only in the `Tag:` line. Re-read each skill start-to-finish for numbering.\n- **Files**: all three SKILL.md \u2014 read only\n- **Verify**: `grep -n 'Commit this? (yes / edit / no)' skills/*/SKILL.md` returns exactly three\n  hits; surrounding bullet text is identical in all three.\n\n### Risks & Unknowns\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Three-way drift is the main risk.** This adds a second verbatim-duplicated block to files\n  that already carry one, with no test to catch divergence. Step 7 exists solely for this.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Renumbering breaks cross-references.** `workflow-commit-dev` step 11 points at step 8 by\n  number; `workflow-release` \u00a73 points forward at \"section 6\". Both must still resolve afterwards.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Prompt changes are unverifiable except by use.** There is no test suite here. The real\n  check is running `/workflow-commit` on this branch's own changes \u2014 which Step 1 makes possible\n  immediately, and is the cheapest end-to-end proof available.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **DW-2 overlap.** Both branches edit `README.md`, and both edit the `version` line of\n  `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` (DW-3 \u2192 1.7.2, DW-2 \u2192 1.8.0). Expect a conflict in both files\n  whichever merges second; the version line resolves in favour of the higher number.\n- \u2753 **Does an `edit` answer need to survive `--breaking`?** If the developer rewrites a subject\n  that carried `!`, the footer may no longer match. Suggest keeping the footer and warning once,\n  but confirm during implementation.\n- \u2753 **`AskUserQuestion` vs plain prompt.** The gate is written as a text prompt for portability\n  to non-Claude agents. In Claude Code an `AskUserQuestion` menu would render better but is not\n  portable. Written as text; worth revisiting if it reads poorly in practice.\n\n### Dependencies\n- None external.\n- Steps 1\u20133 are independent and may be done in any order, but the gate text must be finalised\n  before the first insertion \u2014 otherwise later refinements have to be back-ported to earlier files.\n- Steps 4\u20135 depend on the final gate wording from Steps 1\u20133.\n- Step 7 runs last, after all three insertions.\n\n## Implementation Status\n\n**Completed**: 2026-08-22\n**Steps completed**: 7 of 7\n**Blocked steps**: none\n\n### Changes Made\n- `skills/workflow-commit/SKILL.md` \u2014 gate added as step 8, after the dry-run gate; no `Tag:`\n  line. Steps renumbered 8\u21929, 9\u219210, 10\u219211; step 9 now reads \"Commit with the **approved**\n  message\".\n- `skills/workflow-commit-dev/SKILL.md` \u2014 gate added as step 10, with a `Tag:` line plus a note\n  requiring the **resolved** value (`dev-rc2-1.6.0`), never the `dev-{VersionValue}` template.\n  Steps renumbered 10\u219211 \u2026 13\u219214. Step 12's \"command from step 8\" still resolves \u2014 tag\n  determination sits before the gate and was deliberately not renumbered.\n- `skills/workflow-release/SKILL.md` \u2014 new `#### Step 1 \u2014 Approve the message and tag` opening\n  \u00a76; existing Steps 1/2/3 shifted to 2/3/4. The `git tag -l` availability check was moved here\n  from \u00a77 so the gate cannot advertise a tag that is about to fail. The gate states outright\n  that it is the only chance to change the subject, since Step 3 amends with `--no-edit`.\n- `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 paragraph added to the COMMIT HELPERS block; `commit`,\n  `commit-dev`, and `release` rows of the per-step table now mention the approval.\n- `README.md` \u2014 \"**Nothing commits unapproved.**\" paragraph added after **Commit format**.\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 version 1.7.1 \u2192 1.7.2. Root `plugin.json` untouched (absent on\n  this branch).\n\n### Verification\n- The gate appears in exactly the three intended skills.\n- Step numbering is contiguous: commit 1\u201311, commit-dev 1\u201314, release \u00a76 Steps 1\u20134.\n- Five distinctive gate phrases each appear exactly three times, so the copies have not drifted.\n- The `Tag:` line is present in the `commit-dev` and `release` gates and absent from `commit`.\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` parses as valid JSON.\n- Diff totals 6 files, +128/\u221215.\n\n### Issues Encountered\n- The two open questions from the plan were both settled in the wording rather than left\n  hanging: the `edit` path warns once when an edit conflicts with an enforced convention and\n  takes it anyway, and the gate is a plain text prompt rather than an `AskUserQuestion` menu,\n  so it travels to non-Claude agents.\n- Nothing here is exercised by a test \u2014 this repo has none. The gate is unproven until\n  `/workflow-commit` is run against this branch's own diff, which is now possible and is the\n  cheapest end-to-end check available.\n\n## Release\n\n**Version**: 1.7.2\n**Date**: 2026-08-22\n**Tag**: v1.7.2\n**Pushed**: yes \u2014 `origin/main` (ce3d4e4..8c2898d) and tag `v1.7.2`\n\n### Commit\n- `8c2898d` \u2014 chore(DW-3): release v1.7.2 \u2014 add commit message approval gate (tagged v1.7.2)\n- `3ac3517` \u2014 fix(DW-3): add commit message approval gate to all commit skills\n\n### Notes\n- The gate proved itself twice before being released. On `3ac3517` it surfaced a real\n  inconsistency \u2014 a `feat` subject against a patch bump \u2014 and the `edit` path carried the\n  correction through verbatim instead of forcing a re-run. On this release commit it ran again,\n  this time from `/workflow-release`'s own copy.\n- Detection stayed inconclusive both times (3/12, then 4/13 conventional subjects). The repo is\n  mid-transition from `TASK-ID:` to Conventional Commits, so the style question fires every run\n  by design until conventional subjects clear 60%.\n- Released from `main` at `3ac3517`; DW-2 remains unmerged, so root `plugin.json` is still\n  absent here and 1.8.0 is still free for it.\n"
